http: send the custom headers only to their origin

The headers option carries what a caller adds to its requests, and a
player's are often secret: a media server's token (X-Plex-Token,
X-Emby-Token, Authorization) or a Cookie line. http sent them with every
request of the context, also after a redirect to another host, and the
hls demuxer copies them (ffio_copy_url_options) to every playlist,
segment and key request it opens, whatever the host: a token for the
media server reached the CDN its segments are on, an ad server's
segments stitched into a live playlist, and whatever a redirect named.
Browsers, curl and ExoPlayer send such headers to the origin they were
given for only.

Add the option headers_origin, scheme://host[:port]. The custom headers
go with a request only when its URL has that origin (scheme, host and
port, the scheme's default port when none is given; an IPv6 host in
brackets, http://[::1]:8096; the scheme is part of it, so a redirect from
http://host to https://host is to another origin), checked on every
request: the first, each redirect hop (a hop back to the origin gets
them again), a reconnect, and a new request on a kept-alive connection.
Without the option the origin is that of the URL opened (before any
redirect), worked out at the open and, for headers that came later (a new
request on a kept-alive connection may bring them), when the request is
sent; a redirect to another origin drops them. It fails closed: when no
origin can be read (an unreadable headers_origin or URL, one without the
scheme such as host:8096, any scheme but http and https), the custom
headers go with no request, and a warning says so once per connection.
ffio_copy_url_options() passes the option
on with the headers, so the connections hls (and dash) open send the
headers only to the origin of the URL the input was opened with, or to
the one the caller named: a player that opens an external track with
the media's options names the media's origin, and the track's own host
gets none. Without custom headers nothing changes.

user_agent and referer are not secret and are sent as before. cookies
are left as they are: a cookie is scoped by its own Domain attribute, a
cookie without one goes to every host as before (cookies set by a CDN's
answers need that); a secret cookie should come as a Cookie line in
headers, which is scoped now.

diff --git a/doc/protocols.texi b/doc/protocols.texi
--- a/doc/protocols.texi
+++ b/doc/protocols.texi
@@ -440,6 +440,16 @@ set HTTP proxy to tunnel through e.g. http://example.com:1234
 Set custom HTTP headers, can override built in default headers. The
 value must be a string encoding the headers.
 
+@item headers_origin
+The origin (@samp{scheme://host[:port]}) the custom headers are sent to.
+A request to any other origin, a redirect's included, goes without them.
+Default: the origin of the URL opened. The scheme is part of the origin:
+a redirect from @samp{http://host} to @samp{https://host} is to another
+origin. An IPv6 host is written in brackets. A value that is no origin (one
+without the scheme) sends the headers nowhere, with a warning. The hls
+demuxer passes it on with the headers to the connections it opens, so a
+playlist's segments on another host get no headers either.
+
 @item content_type
 Set a specific content type for the POST messages or for listen mode.
 
